Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.37,0:00:05.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Set {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} at zero point nine, and\Nlaunch two copies, one millionth apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.23,0:00:11.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We know the state to six decimals.\NCan we predict the date of the next flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.04,0:00:17.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At first, the copies move as one.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.92,0:00:21.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But their gap nearly triples\Nevery eleven time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.87,0:00:25.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}After about a hundred and fifty,\Nthey disagree completely.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.31,0:00:30.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The average wait between crossings\Nis longer: over two hundred.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.01,0:00:35.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each extra decimal buys only\Nabout twenty-five more time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.25,0:00:41.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the forecast fades before the average\Nwait ends. Only the statistics survive.
